I'm Leslie curry from the Yale School of  Public Health welcome back to our series  on qualitative research methods this  module addresses qualitative data  analysis we'll look at the principles  and practices of this method just to  remind us the goal of this series is to  enhance our capacity to conceptualize  design and conduct qualitative research  in the health sciences there are six  modules in this series this module  addresses an overview of qualitative  data analysis I want to spend a moment  with this figure which I think conveys  the most essential defining feature of  qualitative data analysis it's an  iterative process of individual and  group level review and interpretation of  narrative data so we move back and forth  between the individual analyst reading  and digestion and interpretation of the  narrative data and then the research  team multidisciplinary research team  interpretation of the data and the  moving back and forth between these  levels is really critical to generating  rich interpretive analysis let's start  with an important definition what are  codes a code is a word or short phrase  that represents the essence or the key  attribute of narrative or verbal  information so for instance think back  to the module where I described a study  on hospitals who take good care of  patients who have heart attacks and  we're interested in what those hospitals  do in order to have great outcomes for  patients with heart attacks we may find  that the support of senior management  within the organization is really  critical a code might be senior  management support these codes are used  to codify or to categorize the data  coding the active coding is the process  of organizing the data into chunks that  are  like chunks or segments of the data that  are similar in important ways and then  codes are developed into a code  structure so what is a code structure  code structure is a compilation of the  emerging codes the codes that are  developed during that process of  individual and group review and  interpretation codes the code structure  includes brief definitions or properties  for each code and the code structure can  also contain illustrative quotes or  excerpts from the transcripts that  demonstrate that particular property or  definition for a code the code structure  might also include guidance for the  coders the analysts for when and how to  use the codes and really important to  recognize that the code structure will  evolve throughout the analysis we often  have 10 or 20 versions of a code  structure in the course of a given  research study so let's take a look at  an example of a code structure this is  from a study conducted by one of the  clinical scholars and the Robert Wood  Johnson clinical scholars program where  I get to teach and be a mentor  Peggy Chan was interested in the  professional experiences of  international medical graduates who are  practicing primary care in the United  States this is a topic very well-suited  for qualitative inquiry what are the  perspectives and experiences of this  very unique population and so Peggy  conducted in-depth interviews with a  sample of international medical  graduates and we had a multidisciplinary  team on the group really very important  I think to the quality of analysis here  we had an organizational psychologist  another international medical graduate  and individual of expertise in primary  care workforce issues so very  interesting broad range of perspectives  on the team doing the analysis we'll  look at the code structure from Peggy's  study this is an example of what a code  structure looks like it's very  hierarchical it looks like an outline  you might do for a research paper the  code structure labeled understanding the  experiences of immigrant physicians  really important to put the research  question embed it right into the code  structure this helps the team remember  where is it that we're going what is the  target what is the intended research  question that we're setting about to  answer with this analysis this helps  protect against drifting off and some  you know interesting new development  that you see emerge in the data but  that's not exactly relevant to the  research question at hand so hurt here  her question what are the challenges and  advantages that international medical  graduates physicians face in providing  patient care in the US healthcare system  and then you see the tags codes labels  enumerated here each with numbers so  first a first set of codes is the notion  of support and these are followed by sub  codes so these are dimensions of this  larger bucket of support so here we  might see colleagues and peers as a  source of support mentors aspects of the  training and curriculum that are  supportive and so code structures look  this way where there are sort of large  conceptual domains tags or labels and  then sub domains that further  differentiate that property you see the  different levels of codes that can be  done some relatively straightforward  support and others more nuanced and  complex so let's just take a look at  number two  this notion that medicine is different  in the US and this is the focus on  professional experiences here some of  the dimensions of this code are that the  culture of medicine is different the  u.s. in the u.s. patient demands for  technology million-dollar workups these  are some ideas related to culture of  medicine the nature of physician patient  interactions what those look like views  around patient autonomy and  decision-making and how the IMG  international medical graduates  experience that authority relationships  in the workplace so you see there's a  range of depth and complexity to codes  within a code structure so there are  three approaches to developing a code  structure the first is a purely  inductive grounded method so really  working wholly from the participant  experiences that drive the analysis  entire  we do line-by-line de novo coding  meaning every line of the transcript is  coded for an emerging idea  nothing is brought brought in advance to  the coding process this may employ  constant comparisons which is the  process of moving back and forth from  the codes to the emerging data to look  to see whether or not there are  similarities or differences and  developing the code structure to  accommodate new ideas the benefit of a  purely inductive grounded method is that  it limits the possibility of the  researcher forcing a preconceived result  coming to the research thinking well  it's all about Authority relationships  here the de novo line by line grounded  practice preempts that possibility the  second approach is a start list method  this process brings a preliminary  organizing framework to the table this  is somewhat deductive in nature because  we're coming with some organizing  scaffolding to the code structure the  initial codes are drawn from things like  the researchers own expertise what is  known about the particular topic of  inquiry the existing evidence base and  so we may come to the study with a  recognition that we anticipate certain  core concepts or codes will be in those  data and so we come with what we call a  start list our team described something  that's sort of a compromise between  these two methods a more pragmatic  approach that we call the integrated  approach  so this retains the benefits of  inductive coding so we want to be very  attentive to what emerges from the data  and yet it acknowledges that certain  code types are useful in developing  certain forms of output and so if we're  thoughtful and deliberate and we bring a  framework to the code structure we're  able to be more precise and efficient in  the code process so we begin with broad  code types and then we develop the sub  codes from the data so I mentioned in  the first slide in our first schematic  we saw the iteration between individual  and group level review we just walk  briefly through this  maduk to illustrate again the cyclical  process of coding and qualitative  analysis so we have our transcript that  enters the system and first and this is  really the most fun part is to always  read the transcript freely before coding  so sitting with nothing other than your  eyes and brain and attention and reading  the data broadly and without any biasing  or assumptions or jumping ahead to  analysis or coding spending time in the  data we then move to create the initial  codes and properties so like in Peggy's  code structure we saw support and the  various properties are defining elements  of this notion of support we bring that  initial code structure to coding two or  three of the transcripts within the data  set a cluster of them and here we're  negotiating and revising the code  structure as we move back and forth  across the individual and group level  reviews and debating and revising the  codes in order to accommodate various  perspectives in the analysis we then  bring the code structure through the  cycle using batches of transcripts to  analyze and constantly negotiating and  revising the code structure throughout  the course of data collection ultimately  the goal is to create a final code  structure that then is applied to the  whole data set systematically so that we  end with a comprehensive fully coded set  of narrative data that we can then begin  analyzing and even more complex levels  so that's an overview of the process of  coding come back to - Peggy's project in  a code structure and I want to give you  a snapshot of a real live document this  was my commenting on Peggy's code  structure one of the very early versions  and I mentioned we often develop a dozen  or more of these through the process and  this is intended this is the richness of  analysis and we're in fact generating  our findings as we go and so in a very  early version of Peggy's code structure  we have the title the research question  and then the support network  professional choices was the original  second code rather than medicine is  different in the United States so in  this very  first version of the code I offer up  some comments over here and my first and  it is always my first comment too many  codes and I usually soften this with a  smiley face because it's a comment I  give all of my students or trainees who  often will come with a code structure of  80 or a hundred codes very diligently  developed through the analysis of a set  of transcripts it's way too many to  navigate ultimately the distillation is  the job of the analyst and we need to  get there sooner rather than later and  working with a hundred list list of 100  codes is just untenable and actually not  productive and so we often will advise  we want about one single page  single-spaced  a number of eight to ten maximum codes  and usually we you know we get there as  the larger codes merge and are refined  over time but it's important not to  start with with way too many codes and  another consideration just deciding the  goals reminding ourselves again of the  focus of the analysis so that the codes  are deliberate and will inform the the  primary research question so just to  give you a flavor of what an early code  structure looks like so I think this  illustration sort of reminds us that we  need to be flexible in refining the code  structure and so we come after our  individual analysis we come to groups  where we need to have negotiations about  well what did you see in that data well  I didn't really see that they were  talking about Authority and  decision-making I saw it more as he was  describing anxiety around the patient  physician communication dynamic and so  those negotiations happen amongst the  team in those negotiations codes may  need to be expanded to encompass more  ideas you might have to refine the  properties of a code you know so it by  this code we're referring to this kind  of you know power dynamic we're not  referring to physician panic patient  interactions you may need to merge into  existing codes and so you're always  looking for logic and flow within the  code structure and being open to the  breaking out or the merging of existing  code  using software for data management this  is becoming sort of a standard in the  field there are many types of software  now for qualitative data management when  I first was trained in the 1990s we used  colored pens and color coded data spread  out over a long table we're much more  efficient these days and using the  software is really seen as as a standard  in the papers that I review in the  grants that I review I expect to see  that the team is using some kind of  software it's really essential in order  to organize large amounts of data and to  be able to manipulate those data in ways  to really enrich the analysis using  software also supports transparency in  the research process and I think this is  really very critical as qualitative  research becomes more and more embraced  in the Health Sciences there's a healthy  skepticism you know what is this process  of analysis what do you mean you know  you code and you develop themes and  having the data organized in an  accessible data set for others to be  able to access and interpret from does  support this broad goal of transparency  which i think really is very important  to the field and an important caution my  friend rhema Yetta who actually trained  me in the 90s and the first software  that was being developed then cautions  us always that software can support the  analysis but it does not do the analysis  for you so it's really only as good as  the analytic team is only as good as the  healthy negotiation and debate in the in  the team is that the software is really  simply a mechanism and a tool for  supporting those those analytic  processes so to close with a few  comments first probably the most  important caveat I can offer up  qualitative data analysis is not easy or  fast  I have students and trainees who think  I'm going to do a you know a study of 20  interviews and  going to finish it in you know a month  and a half and I often have to set some  different expectations the process of  qualitative data analysis is labor and  time intensive it requires  multidisciplinary teamwork where people  feel empowered to challenge and debate  and negotiate and this takes time it's  best accomplished with a team team of  people who have different perspectives  and bring a different lens to the data  the data collection and analysis  processes are iterative so we need to  sort of take off our quantitative  orientation hats where you know we never  dig to the data until they're complete  and we're ready to run our regression  models but here we're collecting the  data we're reading it we're collecting  more data we're reading it's really very  different sort of orientation the common  approach to analysis is the constant  comparative method I mentioned this is  the process of working back and forth  between the emerging data and the  existing code structure to make sure  that everything is accommodated within  the code structure this process does  evolve inductively throughout the course  of the study they did mention we often  do a dozen or more versions of the code  structure and it's important to document  the process and to develop efficient  procedures for coded dating and  management for data coding and  management because again to this point  of transparency the process is long  complex multifaceted and for ensuring  that we can describe the process or  accurately in our manuscripts as well as  make the study methods more transparent  to consumers of the data we want to be  very very careful about creation of  audit trails or these documentation of  processes and studies so thank you for  your attention

